00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.220
Here is how it works when the market sits inside a company.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.340
A corporate prediction market is one the firm runs among employees, so their trades can

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:46.200
put a price on an internal question. A 2017 paper in MIS quarterly models two inputs

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.440
to that price. One is how precise the public information is. The other is whether the people

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.180
trading interact with each other. Sharper public information does not always make the price

00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:01.460
more accurate. The authors call the failure the hidden profiles effect. Aggregation in

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:06.680
their model lays a larger than efficient weight on information that is already public. Evidence

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.280
Maintenance only some participants hold gets too little weight.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.240
That undisclosed evidence is the hidden profile.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.140
Add information sharing and the picture changes.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.260
A socially embedded market may offset the over-weighting and perform better.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:24.500
The model also finds conditions where making public information more precise is harmful with

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:28.500
no network among traders, and harmful again when a network is present.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:30.320
Either design can be hurt.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.460
What they draw for managers is that public information can damage the market, and that

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:39.380
employees' social networks are part of performance, not a distraction from it.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.360
Read that way, a confident price can still be tilted toward the figure everyone already

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:44.740
had.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.620
Usefulness depends on whether private information can move the price, and on whether the

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.340
people who hold it share what they know.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.540
The paper is an analytical model.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:54.940
It names a failure mode.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:57.400
It does not score any particular company.

